  "title": "Nana",
  "media_type": "movie",
  "structured_data": {},
  "metadata_synthesis": "1926 French silent drama. Directed by Jean Renoir. Adapted from Émile Zola's novel. A visually lavish but narratively scrambled literary adaptation.",
  "setting_and_subculture": "19th-century Parisian high society. Lavish, three-dimensional interior sets. A world of aristocratic extravagance and moral rot.",
  "visual_tone_and_feel": "Elegant impressionism. Expansive photography. Static frames. Strikingly lit. A purely imagetic art form.",
  "media_era_tone": "1920s French silent cinema. Ambitious. A collision between Zola's brutal moralism and Renoir's breathing naturalism.",
  "pacing_and_structure": "Truncated. Scrambled. Jumps straight to the decay. A steady parade of men marching toward ruin. Careful framing punctuated by wild cinematic dazzle.",
  "atmosphere_and_mood": "Lavish but doomed. Creeping damnation. Breathtaking beauty masking absolute social decay. Suffocating.",
  "themes_and_messages": "The destructive nature of desire. High society collapsing under low morality. Damnation. The ruinous cost of obsession.",
  "character_relationships": "A courtesan and her doomed suitors. Men tromping through an expensive life. A cycle of obsession, exploitation, and financial ruin.",
  "lead_character_type": "An extravagant courtesan. A destructive force of nature. An actress lacking talent but armed with pure chaotic allure.",
  "story_and_plot_type": "Tragic melodrama. The rise and fall of a courtesan. A social critique veiled as a tragedy of manners.",
  "who_and_when": "French aristocracy. Late 19th century. Men and women destroying each other in lavish drawing rooms.",
  "emotional_evocation": "Awe at the technical craft. Creeping doom. A detached fascination with the raw power of silent imagery."
}
